Limitations & Eval
This section is disclosed because the synthesis is opinionated and the methodology is non-trivially novel.
Methodology Limitations
•	Manual LLM clustering (Stage 3) is less reproducible than embedding-based methods — a different LLM run might split or merge clusters differently. The thematic finding is robust because corroboration is at the source level, not the cluster level.
•	JD over-representation (23 of 40 rows): severity for JD-derived pains should be discounted ~0.7x relative to user-voiced reviews. Adversarial pass corrects for this implicitly via cross-source diversity scoring.
•	Single-platform over-representation (8 of 40 rows, 20%, Nortridge): inflates apparent severity of vendor-support and UI/UX themes. Theme #3 validation experiment specifically targets this gap.
•	Sparse coverage of major commercial servicing platforms (Black Knight MSP, Mortgage Cadence, McCracken, Sagent, FIS LoanServ): limited public review presence. Corpus relies on JDs and forum aggregates to triangulate.
Eval — Hand-Coded Ground Truth
Five sources were hand-coded by the author before pipeline execution. Pipeline outputs were compared against hand-coded results across three dimensions:

Dimension	Pipeline Matched	Pipeline Failed — Corrected
Verbatim-quote provenance	Every extracted pain included verbatim source quote; no inference-only extractions	—
Pain vs. feature distinction	Proposed solutions captured as proposed_solution, not standalone pains	—
De-duplication	Multi-mention LoanIQ UI complaint correctly collapsed to 1 pain with 4 supporting quotes	—
JD extraction	—	JDs returned empty extractions under strict "user expresses friction" prompt. Fix: route JD rows through Quick Note column (workflow→pain pre-translation). Two-line pipeline change.
JD severity	—	JDs defaulted to "high" severity. Mitigation: cross-source diversity weighting, not severity adjustment, to avoid losing JD signal entirely.
Context tag enum	—	Procurement-shaped pains landed in "other" — enum lacked procurement value. Fix: extend enum based on first 50 outputs, not pre-specified.

What the Pipeline Cannot Do
•	Cannot infer severity from absence — if a pain is omitted from a positive review, pipeline treats it as not-present, not as not-a-pain.
•	Cannot judge source authenticity — vendor-perspective quotes were tagged at ingestion; the pipeline did not infer the source bias. Source labeling remains a human responsibility.
•	Cannot validate findings without follow-up research — adversarial-pass falsifiability tests are designs, not data. The next phase is interviewing (see Recommended Next Steps).

